link2symlink: name the files in PROOT_L2S_DIR after their inode number

Without PROOT_L2S_DIR the file behind a faked hard link and its intermediate
sit next to the link, where the guest lists them. A program that hard links
a directory entry by entry (uv installing wheels out of its cache, pnpm,
cp -al) then links those too: the intermediate fails with EPERM, and the
file behind gets a group of its own inside the first. Hermes's installer
ended there ("failed to hardlink file ... .l2s.tags.py0001: Operation not
permitted"). With PROOT_L2S_DIR they live in one directory outside the
guest's and a listing shows the link alone, so the app sets it.

A name there is "<file name><NNNN>", and the suffix counts up from 0001 to
9999 for each file name. That directory holds the files of every directory
of the guest, so the suffixes of a name common in the uv cache
(__init__.py) run out, and link() fails with EMLINK, once 9999 of them are
linked; every PRoot of the rootfs also tries the same taken suffixes first.
In that directory the suffix now starts at the file's inode number, which
is its own for as long as the file exists, and counts up from there when
it is taken.

5.1.107.102 made the rest of what this patch used to do upstream: slots
are reserved atomically with symlink(2), so two PRoot can't take the same
one and a taken one is never replaced, and linkat(AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW)
links the file behind a faked hard link into its group (find_intermediate).
Files linked before keep working, whatever their suffix: they are found
through their intermediate, and only the last 4 digits of a suffix are
read.

diff --git a/src/extension/link2symlink/link2symlink.c b/src/extension/link2symlink/link2symlink.c
index 34dc24a..fc9a981 100644
--- a/src/extension/link2symlink/link2symlink.c
+++ b/src/extension/link2symlink/link2symlink.c
@@ -787,7 +787,9 @@ static int move_and_symlink_path(Tracee *tracee, Reg sysarg, Reg link_target_sys
 	int status;
 	int link_count;
 	int first_link = 1;
-	int intermediate_suffix = 1;
+	unsigned long long first_suffix = 1;
+	unsigned long long intermediate_suffix;
+	int attempts;
 
 	/* Note: this path was already canonicalized.  */
 	size = read_string(tracee, original, peek_reg(tracee, CURRENT, sysarg), PATH_MAX);
@@ -885,17 +887,28 @@ static int move_and_symlink_path(Tracee *tracee, Reg sysarg, Reg link_target_sys
 		 * moved, then left without any name when the intermediate
 		 * could not be created.  The final file a broken chain left
 		 * behind is not replaced either, and nothing is moved when
-		 * there's no free slot left.  The suffix has 4 digits, so
-		 * up to 9999 files of the same name can be linked at once:
-		 * per directory, or in the whole l2s directory.  */
-		for (intermediate_suffix = 1; ; intermediate_suffix++) {
-			if (intermediate_suffix > 9999)
-				return -EMLINK;
+		 * there's no free slot left.  Up to 9999 slots are tried.
+		 *
+		 * The l2s directory holds the files of every directory, and
+		 * every PRoot of this rootfs uses it at once: counting up
+		 * from 1 there runs out once 9999 "__init__.py" of the uv
+		 * cache are linked, and makes every PRoot try the same taken
+		 * slots first.  The suffix starts at the inode number of the
+		 * file instead, which is its own for as long as it exists
+		 * and stays the same once moved.  It has at least 4 digits,
+		 * like the count, c.f. get_link_count().  */
+		if (get_l2s_directory())
+			first_suffix = statl.st_ino;
 
-			status = snprintf(final, PATH_MAX, "%s%04d.0002", intermediate, intermediate_suffix);
+		for (attempts = 0; ; attempts++) {
+			if (attempts >= 9999)
+				return -EMLINK;
+			intermediate_suffix = first_suffix + attempts;
+
+			status = snprintf(final, PATH_MAX, "%s%04llu.0002", intermediate, intermediate_suffix);
 			if (status < 0 || status >= PATH_MAX)
 				return -ENAMETOOLONG;
-			sprintf(new_intermediate, "%s%04d", intermediate, intermediate_suffix);
+			sprintf(new_intermediate, "%s%04llu", intermediate, intermediate_suffix);
 
 			if (l2s_lstat(final, &statl) == 0)
 				continue;
